    {
      "latin": "quaerō, quaerere, quaesīvī, quaesītum",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "inquire into, investigate",
      "grammar": "verb, third conjugation: in this sense with dē + ablative of the matter",
      "evidence": [
        "Cic. Rosc. Am. 119: <span lang=\"la\">dē morte alicujus</span> (Lewis &amp; Short)",
        "Cic. Verr. 1.27: <span lang=\"la\">dē pecūniīs repetundīs</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "The classical word for an official inquiry: <span lang=\"la\">quaerere dē</span> a death, a theft, a charge. The inquiry itself is <span lang=\"la\">quaestiō</span> (<span lang=\"la\">quaestiōnem habēre dē</span>, see trial). Its everyday senses are to look for and to ask (see look-for)."
    },
    {
      "latin": "investīgō, investīgāre, investīgāvī, investīgātum",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "track down, search out",
      "evidence": [
        "Cic. N.D. 2.158 (Lewis &amp; Short)",
        "Traupman 195, 334",
        "Traupman 195",
        "Traupman 289"
      ],
      "comment": "Properly to track, as a hound follows a scent (Cic. N.D. 2.158); then to trace or find out facts. Traupman gives it, and <span lang=\"la\">investīgātor</span>, for investigate and investigator. See also research."
    },
    {
